      "RWR",
      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"j\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [16, 23, 16]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [23, 16, 23]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[System] Expert first round completed in 4.32 seconds\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\nLet me analyze each read/write operation for the global variable `j` to determine if they can actually be executed.\n\n## Analysis of Read/Write Operations for Variable `j`\n\n### Step 1: Check Control Conditions\n\n**Operation 1: Read of `j` at line 16**\n- This read occurs in `svp_simple_327_001_isr_1`\n- The operation is within a `for` loop: `for (int k = 0; k < NUM; k++)`\n- `k` is initialized to 0 and `NUM` is defined as 5\n- The condition `k < NUM` will be true for k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\n- Therefore, this read operation will execute 5 times\n\n**Operation 2: Write of `j` at line 23**\n- This write occurs in `svp_simple_327_001_isr_2`\n- The operation is within a `for` loop: `for (int k = 0; k < NUM; k++)`\n- `k` is initialized to 0 and `NUM` is defined as 5\n- The condition `k < NUM` will be true for k = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4\n- Therefore, this write operation will execute 5 times\n\n**Operation 3: Read of `j` at line 16 (second occurrence)**\n- This is the same as Operation 1, just listed again in the pattern\n- Same analysis applies - it will execute 5 times\n\n### Step 2: Check Interrupt Status\n\nLooking at the code:\n- There are no `disable_isr()` or `enable_isr()` calls in the entire program\n- Both ISRs (interrupt service routines) can preempt each other based on their priority levels\n- `svp_simple_327_001_isr_1` has priority 1\n- `svp_simple_327_001_isr_2` has priority 2\n\nSince there are no interrupt disabling mechanisms, both ISRs can execute and access the shared variables.\n\n### Step 3: Summary of Analysis\n\nAll read/write operations for variable `j` can be executed:\n- The read at line 16 in `svp_simple_327_001_isr_1` can execute\n- The write at line 23 in `svp_simple_327_001_isr_2` can execute\n- These operations can interleave since both ISRs can preempt each other\n\nThe same applies to variable `i` with its corresponding operations.\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"j\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [16, 23, 16]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [23, 16, 23]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"j\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [16, 23, 16]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n"
