      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [16, 23, 16]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[System] Expert first round completed in 2.86 seconds\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\nLet me analyze each read/write operation for the global variable `i` to determine if the reported atomicity violation is valid.\n\n## Analysis of Read/Write Operations for Variable `i`\n\n### Step 1: Check Control Conditions\n\n**Operation 1: Write to `i` at line 16**\n- Location: `svp_simple_327_001_isr_1`, line 16: `i = j + 1;`\n- Control condition: `for (int k = 0; k < NUM; k++)`\n- Variable tracking: `k` is initialized to 0, `NUM` is defined as 5\n- Execution: The loop will execute 5 times (k=0,1,2,3,4), so this write operation will execute 5 times\n- **Conclusion**: This write operation can be executed\n\n**Operation 2: Read from `i` at line 23**\n- Location: `svp_simple_327_001_isr_2`, line 23: `j = i + 1;`\n- Control condition: `for (int k = 0; k < NUM; k++)`\n- Variable tracking: `k` is initialized to 0, `NUM` is defined as 5\n- Execution: The loop will execute 5 times (k=0,1,2,3,4), so this read operation will execute 5 times\n- **Conclusion**: This read operation can be executed\n\n**Operation 3: Write to `i` at line 16 (second occurrence)**\n- This is the same as Operation 1, just the next iteration of the loop\n- **Conclusion**: This write operation can be executed\n\n### Step 2: Check Interrupt Status\n\nLooking at the code:\n- There are no `disable_isr()` or `enable_isr()` calls in the entire program\n- Both ISRs (svp_simple_327_001_isr_1 and svp_simple_327_001_isr_2) can preempt each other based on their priorities (1 and 2 respectively)\n- The main function does not disable any interrupts\n\n### Step 3: Summary of Analysis\n\nAll read/write operations for variable `i` can be executed:\n- Write at line 16 (in isr_1) can execute\n- Read at line 23 (in isr_2) can execute\n- Write at line 16 (in isr_1, next iteration) can execute\n\nThe reported atomicity violation pattern [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"] with lines [16, 23, 16] represents a valid scenario where:\n1. isr_1 writes to `i` at line 16\n2. isr_2 reads from `i` at line 23\n3. isr_1 writes to `i` again at line 16\n\nThis can occur due to interrupt preemption between the two ISRs.\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [16, 23, 16]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"i\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Write\", \"Read\", \"Write\"],\n            \"lines\": [16, 23, 16]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n"
